{%- if issue.labels contains "plan" or issue.labels contains "breakdown" %}

## Ticket type: plan

This ticket has the `plan` label (or `breakdown`, its older name). The operator
may have written it from the Plan template: `Vision`, `Context`, `Constraints`,
`Quality bar`, `Artifacts wanted` and `Done when`. Every run on it follows
`Parent tickets`, and the `Plan pipeline` when it has an `Artifacts wanted`
section.

- A plan ticket is never sent back for missing input and has no readiness check.
  Do not use the clarification escape hatch for a thin or unclear description,
  even one with only a `Vision`.
- Turn each open question into a decision: the options, the default you recommend
  and why. Go on with that default, and list every decision under a `Decisions`
  heading where the plan is handed over for review, so the operator answers them
  all in that single review.
- With no box checked under `Artifacts wanted`, or no such section, the plan is a
  plain split into sub-tickets.
- `Constraints` and `Quality bar` bind every sub-ticket: repeat the ones that
  apply in its description. `Done when` is what the whole must achieve: carry it
  into the `Final verification:` sub-ticket's checklist.
{%- elsif issue.labels contains "type:bug" %}

## Ticket type: bug

This ticket has the `type:bug` label. The operator wrote it from the Bug
template: `Observed`, `Expected`, `Steps to reproduce`, `Where` (platform,
version) and `Evidence`.

### Readiness check

Right after the workpad exists, before planning, check that the description has
each required section with text of the operator's own: `Observed`, `Expected`
and `Steps to reproduce`. A section is missing when its heading is absent, or when
it is empty or holds only the template's italic hint. `Where` and `Evidence` help
but are not required. Skip the check when a PR is already attached to the ticket.

When a required section is missing, do not guess: use the in-execution
clarification escape hatch with one comment that names every missing section and
asks the operator to fill them in and move the ticket back to `Todo`. Record it in
the workpad, move the ticket to `Backlog` and stop, without a branch, a commit or
a PR.

### What a bug run does

1. First, write a test that reproduces the bug: it follows `Steps to reproduce`,
   asserts the `Expected` behaviour and fails on the current code the way
   `Observed` describes. Run it and record the failing command and its output in
   the workpad `Notes` as the reproduction signal, before changing the code under
   test.
2. When the steps do not reproduce the bug on the current default branch, do not
   fix blind: use the clarification escape hatch with one comment saying what you
   ran, on which commit, and what you saw. When no automated test can reach the
   bug (it needs a device or a real key), record the closest deterministic signal
   instead and say why in the workpad.
3. Then fix the cause. The same test now passes and stays in the PR, next to the
   fix, as the regression test.
4. Name the root cause in the workpad `Notes` and in the PR's `## This PR`.
{%- elsif issue.labels contains "type:feature" %}

## Ticket type: feature

This ticket has the `type:feature` label. The operator wrote it from the Feature
template: `Goal`, `Context`, `Acceptance criteria`, `User walkthrough` and
`Out of scope`.

### Readiness check

Right after the workpad exists, before planning, check that the description has
each required section with text of the operator's own: `Goal` and
`Acceptance criteria` (at least one criterion). A section is missing when its
heading is absent, or when it is empty or holds only the template's italic hint.
`Context`, `User walkthrough` and `Out of scope` help but are not required. Skip
the check when a PR is already attached to the ticket.

When a required section is missing, do not guess: use the in-execution
clarification escape hatch with one comment that names every missing section and
asks the operator to fill them in and move the ticket back to `Todo`. Record it in
the workpad, move the ticket to `Backlog` and stop, without a branch, a commit or
a PR.

### What a feature run delivers

- One PR that meets every item under `Acceptance criteria`. Copy them into the
  workpad `Acceptance Criteria` as written, and check each one off only with
  evidence: a test, a command's output or a screenshot.
- A test for every criterion that code can check.
- When the ticket has a `User walkthrough`, every step works end to end as
  written, with the result it names.
- Nothing listed under `Out of scope`. Use `Goal` and `Context` to settle details
  the criteria leave open, and file anything larger with `linear_create_subissue`
  instead of building it.
{%- endif -%}
